    "q-32571": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32571",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
